WEBVTT

1
00:00:12.523 --> 00:00:15.744
was fond of the Large family because they looked happy.

2
00:00:16.744 --> 00:00:20.305
But she liked the gentleman next door because he looked unhappy.

3
00:00:21.606 --> 00:00:24.286
The school's servants were always talking about him.

4
00:00:25.607 --> 00:00:29.868
Sara learned that the English gentleman had become ill after losing his fortune.

5
00:00:30.924 --> 00:00:33.746
He'd gotten his fortune back, but he was still sick.

6
00:00:33.766 --> 00:00:38.670
"His problems were connected with diamond mines," said the cook.

7
00:00:40.052 --> 00:00:41.052
She glanced at Sara.

8
00:00:41.072 --> 00:00:43.635
"We all know they're trouble."

9
00:00:43.655 --> 00:00:49.820
"That man was just like Papa," Sara said to herself.

10
00:00:49.840 --> 00:00:54.684
"He felt just what my Papa felt, only he did not die."

11
00:00:56.085 --> 00:00:59.648
So Sara grew even fonder of the sick gentleman next door.

12
00:01:02.028 --> 00:01:05.211
Whenever she was sent out at night, she stopped outside his house.

13
00:01:06.432 --> 00:01:11.556
She liked to peek into the house and see him sitting in his armchair, his forehead resting in

14
00:01:11.596 --> 00:01:12.077
his hand.

15
00:01:12.997 --> 00:01:14.479
She often wished him good night.

16
00:01:15.800 --> 00:01:21.024
"Perhaps you can feel my good wishes even if you can't hear them," she said.

17
00:01:21.044 --> 00:01:24.407
"I hope you'll get well and be happy again.

18
00:01:24.427 --> 00:01:26.709
I'm so sorry for you."

19
00:01:28.731 --> 00:01:32.594
She went on her way, feeling comforted and a bit warmer herself.

20
00:01:33.755 --> 00:01:35.616
But she kept worrying about the man.

21
00:01:36.877 --> 00:01:40.459
He looked to Sara like someone who had a lot of trouble on his mind.

22
00:01:42.100 --> 00:01:43.281
Meanwhile Mr.

23
00:01:43.321 --> 00:01:46.964
Large and his daughters Janet and Nora often visited the gentleman.

24
00:01:48.265 --> 00:01:49.866
The sick man's name was Mr.

25
00:01:49.926 --> 00:01:51.947
Carrisford. Mr.

26
00:01:51.987 --> 00:01:53.528
Large's real name was Mr.

27
00:01:53.588 --> 00:01:55.770
Carmichael. He was Mr.

28
00:01:55.830 --> 00:01:56.911
Carrisford's lawyer.

29
00:01:58.131 --> 00:01:59.913
One evening Janet told Mr.

30
00:01:59.953 --> 00:02:02.635
Carrisford about the Little Girl Who Is Not a Beggar.

31
00:02:04.296 --> 00:02:05.637
He was very interested.

32
00:02:07.719 --> 00:02:10.821
Then Ram Dass told him the story of the monkey's escape.

33
00:02:11.862 --> 00:02:16.785
Ram Dass described the servant girl's cold attic room and her hard, narrow bed.

34
00:02:18.326 --> 00:02:19.687
The story upset Mr.

35
00:02:19.728 --> 00:02:25.532
Carrisford. He was wealthy and comfortable while children were cold and hungry.

36
00:02:27.180 --> 00:02:31.644
"I wonder how many of the attics in London are like that one," he said to Mr.

37
00:02:31.684 --> 00:02:38.690
Carmichael. "How many young servant girls sleep on hard beds while I sleep on soft pillows?"

38
00:02:38.710 --> 00:02:41.713
"My dear fellow," Mr.

39
00:02:41.753 --> 00:02:43.774
Carmichael answered in a cheery voice.

40
00:02:43.794 --> 00:02:47.037
"You must stop thinking thoughts like this.

41
00:02:47.698 --> 00:02:51.701
Even if you had all the wealth in the world, you could not improve all the attics in

42
00:02:51.761 --> 00:02:54.844
London. You must focus your thoughts on becoming well."

43
00:02:56.476 --> 00:03:00.398
Mr. Carrisford sat and bit his nails as he stared into the fire.

44
00:03:00.418 --> 00:03:05.161
"Do you think the girl I'm looking for is living in such a horrid place?"

45
00:03:05.641 --> 00:03:06.702
he asked suddenly.

46
00:03:07.762 --> 00:03:09.823
Mr. Carmichael looked at him uneasily.

47
00:03:10.964 --> 00:03:15.066
He knew that this kind of thinking was very dangerous to the frail gentleman's health.

48
00:03:16.587 --> 00:03:19.749
"I don't think so," he said finally in a soothing tone.

49
00:03:20.809 --> 00:03:23.290
"That girl was adopted by a wealthy Russian couple."

50
00:03:23.310 --> 00:03:25.852
"I must find her!"

51
00:03:26.268 --> 00:03:27.669
Mr. Carrisford went on.

52
00:03:27.689 --> 00:03:31.931
"If she is friendless and penniless, it is my fault!

53
00:03:32.652 --> 00:03:35.333
She is the daughter of my dear friend, Captain Crewe."

54
00:03:35.353 --> 00:03:39.796
"Are you sure his daughter was sent to boarding school in France?"

55
00:03:40.236 --> 00:03:41.517
Mr. Carmichael asked.

56
00:03:41.537 --> 00:03:44.339
"Perhaps we're searching for the wrong girl."

57
00:03:44.359 --> 00:03:47.360
"I'm not sure of anything."

58
00:03:47.841 --> 00:03:49.702
Mr. Carrisford bit his nails again.

59
00:03:49.722 --> 00:03:53.344
"Crewe and I talked mainly about the mines.

60
00:03:53.984 --> 00:03:57.266
Her mother was French, so I assumed she went to a French school."

61
00:03:57.286 --> 00:04:03.688
"The French headmistress pronounced the student's name Carew instead of Crewe," Mr.

62
00:04:03.728 --> 00:04:04.929
Carmichael reminded him.

63
00:04:04.949 --> 00:04:09.291
"So it's possible that we are on the trail of the wrong girl."

64
00:04:09.311 --> 00:04:12.352
"We must find Crewe's daughter!"

65
00:04:12.792 --> 00:04:14.373
Mr. Carrisford clutched Mr.

66
00:04:14.393 --> 00:04:15.213
Carmichael's arm.

67
00:04:15.233 --> 00:04:17.134
"Help me find her!"

68
00:04:17.154 --> 00:04:20.436
"Don't worry," said Mr.

69
00:04:20.476 --> 00:04:23.897
Carmichael. "I'll go to Moscow to look for the girl.

70
00:04:24.297 --> 00:04:26.758
If she's not the right girl, we will keep searching."

71
00:04:26.778 --> 00:04:31.621
"If I were able to travel, I would go with you," said Mr.

72
00:04:31.661 --> 00:04:34.162
Carrisford. "But I am too ill.

73
00:04:35.202 --> 00:04:38.524
I can only sit here wrapped in fur and staring at the fire.

74
00:04:40.244 --> 00:04:43.646
And when I look into it, I see Crewe's face gazing back at me.

75
00:04:44.006 --> 00:04:47.528
And he's asking me a question: 'Tom, where is my little girl?'"

76
00:04:49.640 --> 00:04:51.301
Mr. Carrisford grabbed Mr.

77
00:04:51.361 --> 00:04:52.901
Carmichael's hand desperately.

78
00:04:52.921 --> 00:04:55.802
"I must be able to answer him!"

79
00:04:58.482 --> 00:05:03.304
On the other side of the wall, Sara was sitting in her room, talking to Mel the rat.

80
00:05:03.324 --> 00:05:08.325
"It was very hard to be a princess today, Mel," she said.

81
00:05:08.345 --> 00:05:13.246
"It gets harder as the weather gets colder and the streets get wetter."

82
00:05:14.627 --> 00:05:18.708
Lavinia had laughed at Sara's skirt, spattered with mud from the street.

83
00:05:19.980 --> 00:05:21.661
But Sara had not responded.

84
00:05:21.681 --> 00:05:28.884
"You can't snap at people if you're a princess," she explained to the rat.

85
00:05:28.904 --> 00:05:33.146
"But it was such a cold afternoon.

86
00:05:33.166 --> 00:05:35.587
And it's so cold tonight."

87
00:05:36.768 --> 00:05:38.328
Sara put her head in her lap.

88
00:05:38.348 --> 00:05:41.190
"Oh, Papa," she whispered.

89
00:05:41.210 --> 00:05:45.932
"It seems like such a long time since I was your little girl!"
